    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Old Norse, the North Germanic language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>, was spoken from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> to Iceland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and carved in runes on thousands of memorial stones.</li><li>In Iceland, from the 12th to the 14th centuries, it became the language of a great literature, the sagas, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edda">Eddas</a> and skaldic poetry, which preserves the myths and history of the North.</li><li>It is the ancestor of Icelandic, Faroese, Norwegian, Danish and Swedish, and through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> settlement it gave English some of its most everyday words, from sky and egg to they and take.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>English owes some of its most basic words to Old Norse, among them they, them and their, sky, egg, knife, take and window, from vindauga, “wind eye”.</p></aside>
    </section>

