    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/viking-age"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/viking-age.jpg" alt="Viking Age" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bildsten_fr%C3%A5n_Smiss,_Gotland.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">historical museum, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Old Norse belongs to the North Germanic branch of the Germanic languages, the family that also includes English, German and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>. Its ancestor, Proto-Norse, is known from inscriptions written in the older runic alphabet of twenty-four letters, the Elder Futhark, from about 200 CE. One of the most famous, on a golden horn found at Gallehus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and made around 400, reads “I, Hlewagastir of Holt, made the horn”.</p><p>Between the 6th and 8th centuries the language changed rapidly: unstressed vowels were lost, words were shortened, and new vowel sounds appeared through umlaut. By around 750 these changes had produced Old Norse, the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>. At the same time, around 800, the runic alphabet was reduced to the sixteen letters of the Younger Futhark, even though the language now had more sounds than before.</p><p>Old Norse was spoken across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> with only slight regional differences, which grew over time: Old West Norse in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and the lands settled from it, Iceland and the Atlantic islands, and Old East Norse in Denmark and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. Speakers regarded their language as one, and called it dǫnsk tunga, “the Danish tongue”.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> Age, which began with the raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a> in 793, carried this language far beyond Scandinavia. Its speakers raided, traded and settled across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the North Atlantic, and their language took root in new lands, above all Iceland, settled from around 870, where it was to produce its greatest literature.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="structure">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bryggen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bryggen.jpg" alt="Bryggen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bryggen">Bryggen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bryggen,_Bergen,_Noruega,_2019-09-08,_DD_115-117_PAN.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Old Norse was a highly inflected language, like Old English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. Nouns had four cases, nominative, accusative, genitive and dative, three genders and strong and weak forms; adjectives and verbs agreed with them, and verbs changed their vowels to mark the past, as English sing and sang still do. A distinctive innovation was the definite article attached to the end of the noun, so that maðr, “man”, became maðrinn, “the man”, a feature still found in the Scandinavian languages today.</p><p>Its sounds included those written þ, thorn, and ð, eth, the two “th” sounds, which Icelandic still uses, and a rich set of vowels, among them sounds such as y and ø made with rounded lips.</p><p>The Younger Futhark, with only sixteen runes, used one sign for several sounds, so that runic inscriptions can be hard to interpret. Runes were carved on stone memorials, but also on wood, bone and metal for everyday purposes: more than six hundred runic inscriptions on sticks found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bryggen">Bryggen</a>, the old harbour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, from the 12th to the 15th centuries, include business letters, prayers, love messages and jokes.</p><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> came the Latin alphabet, adapted to Norse with the letters þ and ð. In the 12th century an anonymous Icelandic scholar wrote the First Grammatical Treatise, proposing a spelling for Icelandic based on an analysis of its sounds so precise that it anticipates the methods of modern linguistics. Old Norse poetry used alliteration rather than rhyme, and the court poets, the skalds, developed complex metres and kennings, poetic circumlocutions such as “Ymir’s skull” for the sky.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="speakers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Speakers &amp; Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/varangian-guard"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/varangian-guard.jpg" alt="Varangian Guard" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/varangian-guard">Varangian Guard</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_body_of_Leo_V_is_dragged_to_the_Hippodrome_through_the_Skyla_Gate.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> Old Norse speakers settled in much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danelaw">Danelaw</a> of eastern and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Norse and English speakers lived side by side, and hundreds of place names ending in -by, “farm” or “village”, and -thorpe, “outlying settlement”, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derby">Derby</a>, Grimsby, Whitby and Scunthorpe, mark their settlements. Norse kings ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, founded as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> base in 841, and the Isle of Man, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shetland">Shetland</a> a descendant of Old Norse, Norn, was spoken until the 18th century.</p><p>Norse speakers also settled in Normandy, whose name means “land of the Northmen”, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroe Islands</a>, in Iceland, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erik-the-red">Erik the Red</a> founded settlements around 985; around 1000 Norse sailors reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanse-aux-meadows">L’Anse aux Meadows</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a>. In the east, Scandinavians known as Rus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangians">Varangians</a> travelled the rivers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where Varangians served in the emperor’s guard; a Viking named Halfdan carved his name in runes on a balustrade of the gallery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a>.</p><p>Most of the Viking Age runestones were raised in the 10th and 11th centuries, the great majority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, to commemorate the dead. Many record journeys to England, the Baltic, Russia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and Serkland, the Muslim lands, and the expedition of Ingvar the Far-Travelled to the east around 1040.</p><p>In the lands where they settled among other peoples, Norse speakers gradually adopted English, Gaelic or French within a few generations. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, Old Norse developed into separate languages: by the 14th century, the Danish, Swedish and Norwegian of the documents differ clearly from one another, while in Iceland the language changed much less.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="literature">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Literature &amp; Learning</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson.jpg" alt="Snorri Sturluson" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson">Snorri Sturluson</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Snorre_Sturluson-Christian_Krohg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Christian Krohg, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The earliest Old Norse texts are runic inscriptions. The Rök stone in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, raised around 800, bears the longest known runic inscription, an enigmatic text of some 760 runes that alludes to heroic legends and riddles still debated by scholars. The great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelling">Jelling</a> stone in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, raised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-bluetooth">Harald Bluetooth</a> around 965, records his conquest of Denmark and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and the conversion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>. On the marble lion that once stood in the harbour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piraeus">Piraeus</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and now stands before the Arsenal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, Scandinavian visitors carved runes in the 11th century.</p><p>Skaldic poetry, composed by court poets from the 9th century and preserved in later writings, celebrated kings and battles in intricate verse; the Icelandic warrior-poet Egill Skallagrímsson, in the 10th century, mourned the death of his sons in one of its most moving poems. The poems of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edda">Poetic Edda</a> told the myths of the gods and the legends of heroes such as Sigurd the dragon-slayer.</p><p>The great age of Old Norse literature came in Iceland in the 12th to 14th centuries. Ari Thorgilsson wrote the Book of the Icelanders around 1130, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landnamabok">Book of Settlements</a> recorded the families who settled the island, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson">Snorri Sturluson</a> wrote the Prose Edda and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heimskringla">Heimskringla</a>, the history of the kings of Norway, in the early 13th century. The Sagas of Icelanders, prose narratives of the feuds, loves and fates of the families of the settlement age, written in a terse and realistic style, such as Egils saga, Laxdæla saga, Grettis saga and Njáls saga, are among the masterpieces of medieval literature.</p><p>In Norway, the court of King Hákon IV had French romances translated into Norse in the 13th century, beginning with the story of Tristan in 1226, and the King’s Mirror offered advice on statecraft, trade and the natural world. Laws, chronicles, saints’ lives and scientific texts complete one of the richest vernacular literatures of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="change">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Change &amp; Descendants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/edda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edda.jpg" alt="Edda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/edda">Edda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Tree_of_Yggdrasil.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">W. G. Collingwood (1854-1932), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the 14th century Old Norse gave way to its descendants. In Iceland and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroe Islands</a> the language changed relatively little, and Icelandic, protected by its literature and by a deliberate policy of creating new words from native roots, remains so close to Old Norse that Icelanders can read the sagas in the original with modest effort. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, Danish, Swedish and Norwegian developed into separate languages; under Danish rule, written Norwegian was replaced by Danish until the 19th century, when two modern Norwegian standards were created.</p><p>Old Norse left a deep mark on English. Because Norse and English were closely related, speakers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danelaw">Danelaw</a> could understand each other, and English borrowed not only words for new things but basic everyday words, among them sky, skin, egg, knife, leg, window, husband, law, take, give, get, die and ugly, and even the pronouns they, them and their. Hundreds of place names across northern and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, Ireland and Normandy are of Norse origin.</p><p>The rediscovery of Old Norse began in the 17th century, when scholars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> studied runes and Icelandic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. The Icelandic scholar Árni Magnússon, who lived from 1663 to 1730, collected most of the surviving manuscripts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, saving many from loss, though the fire of Copenhagen in 1728 destroyed part of his library.</p><p>After Iceland’s independence, Denmark agreed to return many of the manuscripts. In 1971 the Codex Regius of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edda">Poetic Edda</a> and Flateyjarbók arrived in Reykjavík, welcomed by crowds, and over the following decades some 1,800 manuscripts and fragments followed. The Arnamagnæan collection, divided between Copenhagen and Reykjavík, is listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> as part of the Memory of the World.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/heimskringla"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heimskringla.jpg" alt="Heimskringla" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/heimskringla">Heimskringla</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lbs_fragm_82,_0001r_-_0.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National and University Library of Iceland, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Old Norse literature is one of the glories of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The sagas, with their realistic narratives of violence, law and honour, are often seen as forerunners of the modern novel, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edda">Eddas</a> preserve almost everything that is known of the mythology of the North.</p><p>The languages descended from Old Norse are spoken by more than 20 million people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> and the North Atlantic, and Icelandic preserves its ancient form to a remarkable degree, including the use of patronymics, names formed from the father’s or mother’s name, as in the sagas.</p><p>English, too, is in part a child of Old Norse, which shaped its vocabulary and perhaps the simplification of its grammar. The days of the week, place names across northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the everyday words of English carry the memory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> settlement.</p><p>From Wagner’s operas and Tolkien’s Middle-earth to modern novels, films and games, the language, names and stories of Old Norse continue to inspire, and the study of Old Norse is pursued in universities around the world.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Old Norse, the North Germanic language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>, was spoken from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> to the North Atlantic, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and carved in runes on thousands of memorial stones. In medieval Iceland it became the language of the sagas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edda">Eddas</a>, and it is the ancestor of the Scandinavian languages and the source of many of the most basic words of English.</em></p>

